WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.311
- Hi everyone, welcome
to the Daily Homeroom.

00:00:06.311 --> 00:00:09.160
For those of you who aren't
familiar with what this is

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.530
or might just be showing up
off of Facebook or YouTube,

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:15.410
this is Khan Academy's way
of making sure that we all

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.600
stay connected during school closures.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.570
Obviously Khan Academy,
we're a not for profit,

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.240
mission free, world class
education for anyone anywhere.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.727
We could've never predicted
the situation that we're all

00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:27.337
in now but over the last many years,

00:00:27.337 --> 00:00:30.920
we've been building resources
in math, in science,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.050
in the humanities, in
English and language arts

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.000
and for early learners, that
have been free and are free

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.050
that are not commercial.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.420
They're supported by
philanthropic donations

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.746
from folks like yourself.

00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:45.500
And we've always intended
them to be used in conjunction

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.610
with physical classrooms
so students can learn

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.350
at their own time and
pace and master concepts.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.800
And also used by students
when they're outside

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:53.633
of the classroom.

00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:55.510
Be that tutor that their
family could otherwise

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.145
not afford or if they don't have access

00:00:57.145 --> 00:00:59.260
to certain types of classes.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.390
And it's translated into
the languages of the world

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.290
so we could also imagine
there's parts of the planet

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:04.950
that don't have access to schooling

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.390
or the types of schooling
that we would want

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.360
for our own children.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.010
And that's where Khan
Academy, we've also seen

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:10.843
playing a role.

00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:12.640
But when the school
closures started happening,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.975
we realized it's our duty
to step up and to do more.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.357
And so we wanna provide
more supports for all of you

00:01:18.357 --> 00:01:21.080
students, teachers and parents.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.917
We've been running webinars
for parents and teachers

00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:26.210
to try to understand, how do
you navigate school closures?

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.242
How do you do this somewhat
ad hoc homeschooling

00:01:29.242 --> 00:01:30.471
all of a sudden?

00:01:30.471 --> 00:01:33.670
On top of that we've been
doing this daily livestream

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:34.940
which is really just a place to talk

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.670
about anything, stay connected.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.630
It could be of topics
of interest and topics

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:39.700
that you're struggling with.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.210
I encourage everyone whether
you're watching on Facebook

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.660
or on YouTube, we're always
learning about new ways

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:45.637
people are watching this,

00:01:45.637 --> 00:01:47.907
put questions on the message
board, have team members

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.670
who are going to be surfacing them to us.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.630
And we're also going to have,
I'm going to introduce them

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.600
in a second, some experts
on child psychology.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.450
Especially for students
with special learning needs.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.070
So definitely put those questions as well.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.644
But we'll answer all and any questions.

00:02:00.644 --> 00:02:04.530
I do wanna make a special
shout out to all of you

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.790
who've helped support Khan Academy.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.760
Not only over the years
but especially over

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:08.779
the last few weeks.

00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:13.654
We are a nonprofit that runs
through philanthropic donation.

00:02:13.654 --> 00:02:17.091
We were running at a deficit
even before this crisis had hit

00:02:17.091 --> 00:02:19.830
and as you can imagine
over the last few weeks,

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:23.810
our server utilization is 2x,
3x of what it typically is.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.024
So we need that support
even more now than ever.

00:02:26.024 --> 00:02:31.024
A special thanks to Bank
of America, AT&amp;T, Novartis

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.840
and Google.org for being some
of the first corporations

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.384
to step up but we need more.

00:02:35.384 --> 00:02:38.940
We're still running at a deficit.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.550
So definitely get those
questions, questions literally

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.010
about anything, I like some
of the fun and frivolous ones

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.416
as well and they can also be
serious ones about education

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:48.970
or about the coronavirus or whatever.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.595
But with that, I'd like to introduce

00:02:50.595 --> 00:02:52.779
our two guests for today.

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:57.779
We have Laura Phillips who's
a clinical psychologist,

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.882
a neuro psychologist I should say,

00:03:00.882 --> 00:03:02.390
at the Child Mind Institute.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.340
And we also have Dave
Anderson who I believe

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.244
is going to be joining us as well.

00:03:06.244 --> 00:03:09.226
Laura, maybe a good place to start is,

00:03:09.226 --> 00:03:13.140
what are you seeing out there
now that a lot of you know,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.150
we have this forced social
distancing, what do you see

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:17.833
as the major issues that
parents are coping with,

00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:19.840
that students are coping with?

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.586
- Sure, so parents have
had to turn their homes

00:03:22.586 --> 00:03:24.630
into their kid's classrooms.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.612
And my patient population tends to be kids

00:03:27.612 --> 00:03:30.784
who are struggling with
learning disorders or ADHD.

00:03:30.784 --> 00:03:34.180
And this is challenging
for all students now

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.380
to have to pivot and shift their learning

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.627
to their home environment

00:03:37.627 --> 00:03:39.790
but it's particularly challenging for kids

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.752
with learning disabilities
who really do need

00:03:41.752 --> 00:03:46.290
explicit, teacher directed,
recursive learning approaches

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.030
but just aren't feasible right
now through remote learning.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.260
So parents are really
having to step up in a way

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.511
that they've never needed
to before and provide

00:03:55.511 --> 00:03:58.520
the support that teachers
were traditionally

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.760
providing their students
without any training.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.186
And it's a really big ask.

00:04:02.186 --> 00:04:04.220
I say to all the parents with whom I work,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.550
it doesn't matter how bright
you are, what a rock star

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:08.690
you are at your job, how
well you know your students

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:11.010
and your student's learning
profile, how well you parent

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.470
your individual student,

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.560
teaching them is a very different thing.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.870
And a lot of what teachers
actually end up doing

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.690
in the classroom is
really behavior management

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:22.245
and modifying the way that
they present information

00:04:22.245 --> 00:04:25.030
in a way that makes it
engaging and appealing

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:25.870
to their students.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.650
And parents now have to
be the ones doing that

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.860
without the training and the experience

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.270
that their teachers do.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.217
So a lot of the questions
I'm getting really is about,

00:04:35.217 --> 00:04:37.650
"How do I keep my student engaged?

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.410
How do I manage my student's frustration?

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:43.224
And how do I manage newly
occurring or historic,

00:04:43.224 --> 00:04:45.894
long-standing oppositional
behaviors in the context

00:04:45.894 --> 00:04:48.990
of frustrating academic situations?"

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.490
- And so what are these techniques?

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.597
What are some you know, I've
experienced some of that

00:04:53.597 --> 00:04:55.360
in my own household.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.810
I think it's for all students,

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.760
not just special needs
students but some of this

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.590
might be more pronounced
with those students.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.416
What are some tangible
things you would tell parents

00:05:02.416 --> 00:05:06.630
who maybe are having trouble
keeping their kids motivated

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.078
or engaged right now?

00:05:08.078 --> 00:05:11.338
- Well, the first is to
cut yourself some slack

00:05:11.338 --> 00:05:14.348
and to cut your kids some slack
because this is really hard.

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:17.560
No one has ever needed to
do this with the exception

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.810
of parents who opted to
homeschool their kids

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.130
before this occurred.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:25.203
But this is a completely
unprecedented and challenging

00:05:25.203 --> 00:05:28.230
and stressful situation
for kids and for parents

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.370
who are having to balance
the responsibilities

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.030
of working from home
and raising their kids

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:33.910
and now schooling them.

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:36.203
So the first thing is really
cut yourself some slack.

00:05:36.203 --> 00:05:38.280
This is not the time to
strive for perfection.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.163
And the most important
thing really is to maintain

00:05:41.163 --> 00:05:44.835
physical and mental health right now.

00:05:44.835 --> 00:05:47.440
And after that,
communication with teachers

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:48.800
is really critical.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.906
A lot of the students with
whom I work have a lot

00:05:50.906 --> 00:05:52.991
of either environmental modifications

00:05:52.991 --> 00:05:57.230
or school work modifications
that the teachers

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:59.825
can really help the parents
understand what level

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:02.330
of independence they should be expecting

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.350
with their students, how
much time should they expect

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.020
their students to be able
to work independently

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.640
on a task without a teacher,
a parent stepping in

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.610
and either prompting
or checking in on them.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:15.540
Or some students might even need a parent

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.390
sitting next to them the entire time

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:18.240
to keep themselves on task.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.530
But the teacher's gonna be
the best person to inform

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:24.470
what the child's capabilities are.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.650
That's gonna vary depending
on the child's age,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.850
depending on their
child's learning profile.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.070
So again, I would really
turn to the teachers

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.460
and partner with the teachers
so that you really understand

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.090
what your role should be in
supporting your students.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:41.320
And to the extent that you
can, maintain structure

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:46.320
and consistency you know, which
might mean starting the day

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:47.203
at the same time.

00:06:47.203 --> 00:06:48.840
Kids should be I think,
waking up at the same

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.370
that they historically
wake up, having good meals

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.095
at standard times, going
to bed at the same time,

00:06:55.095 --> 00:06:57.610
setting your kids up
for success with respect

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:59.650
to getting sufficient sleep, eating well.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.180
If your children are
prescribed medication,

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:02.450
making sure that they continue to take

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:04.190
their medication as prescribed.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.810
Making sure that they have
the tools that they need

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.760
to complete the online work.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.081
So whether that is computer or laptop,

00:07:11.081 --> 00:07:14.073
knowing how to use the
various online platforms

00:07:14.073 --> 00:07:18.250
that schools are rolling
out and schedules.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.950
Schedules I think, are really,
really critical for students

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:23.640
with anxiety, for students
with ADHD, who have a hard time

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.963
not knowing what's gonna come next.

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:29.410
And yeah, you look like you're

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:30.575
about to pop in with something.

00:07:30.575 --> 00:07:32.210
- And how do you know,
you know as you mentioned,

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:33.645
and I completely understand.

00:07:33.645 --> 00:07:36.010
You mentioned parents
might have to sit down

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.520
next to their student or
they need to understand

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.303
from their teacher but what
we're seeing, in fact I think

00:07:41.303 --> 00:07:43.556
this is the majority
situation is parents are also

00:07:43.556 --> 00:07:45.145
having to work right now.

00:07:45.145 --> 00:07:47.953
And then they're not
getting a lot of information

00:07:47.953 --> 00:07:51.070
in some cases, you know 'cause
teachers are still trying

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.770
to figure out, some districts
are still figuring out

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:53.822
the virtualization plans.

00:07:53.822 --> 00:07:56.773
And in many cases, the
special needs students,

00:07:56.773 --> 00:07:59.160
they haven't even gotten that
far to think about that yet.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.271
So what do you tell those
parents who, they're trying

00:08:02.271 --> 00:08:05.370
to work simultaneously but
you know, their student

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:07.948
might need someone to sit down,
how do they navigate that?

00:08:07.948 --> 00:08:11.092
- So shifts I think are really important.

00:08:11.092 --> 00:08:15.058
Partnering with your partner
and trying to tag team

00:08:15.058 --> 00:08:17.718
so that one person can feel
like they're getting work done

00:08:17.718 --> 00:08:20.370
while supporting their students.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.440
I think that part of co
parenting is really critical

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.120
and I understand that that's not feasible

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.090
for a lot of people but
that's one thing that I think

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.060
can help parents feel as
though they're still keeping up

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:30.893
with their work.

00:08:30.893 --> 00:08:33.030
Although, the reality is no
one's gonna be productive

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:34.830
as they would be under ideal circumstances

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.682
and these aren't ideal circumstances.

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:39.594
But again, I would encourage
parents to really talk

00:08:39.594 --> 00:08:43.454
to teachers and to turn
to resources like you.

00:08:43.454 --> 00:08:47.580
You provide really nice I think,

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.530
complimentary educational
programming that can supplement

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.605
a lot of what schools are rolling.

00:08:53.605 --> 00:08:56.860
I'm finding that schools
really vary in the amount

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:59.966
of direction and structure
that they're providing

00:08:59.966 --> 00:09:02.863
in their rollout of curriculum.

00:09:02.863 --> 00:09:05.375
Some schools have a very strict schedule

00:09:05.375 --> 00:09:08.340
and they have online class
discussions throughout the day,

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.690
other schools have just sent
home packets of information

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:13.531
which can be really overwhelming
for students to sit down.

00:09:13.531 --> 00:09:16.670
So when that's occurring,
I encourage parents really

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:20.700
to do a lot of chunking for
them trying to break down

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.990
these larger packets into
smaller, more manageable chunks

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.570
so that their kids don't feel overwhelmed

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.548
and then avoid the tasks.

00:09:29.548 --> 00:09:33.030
And alternating it with
more appealing activities

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.180
so that they feel like they
have some sort of reward

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.590
at the end of engaging in
something that's really stressful

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:38.903
and frustrating for them.

00:09:38.903 --> 00:09:41.290
- I'm getting some
questions over Facebook,

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.010
one from Elizabeth Handlon.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:45.450
It says, "Any advice on
probing your children

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.410
who aren't innately
talkers but clearly dealing

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:50.107
with difficulty processing?"

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.590
- Processing, I'm not sure that
I understand that question.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:57.533
- Like processing what's going
on in the world right now

00:09:57.533 --> 00:10:00.160
or you know, all the sudden
their school's been closed.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.410
How do they cope with it?

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:03.811
They're not talking to
their friends as much,

00:10:03.811 --> 00:10:06.150
you know, their parents are
stressed, they're watching

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.373
the news, things are going on.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:14.110
- Yeah, so kids do well
when they have information.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.270
Being in the dark is stressful

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:16.750
and anxiety inducing for anybody.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:19.613
So I do encourage parents
to give kids information

00:10:19.613 --> 00:10:22.762
that they're seeking in a
developmentally appropriate way.

00:10:22.762 --> 00:10:26.240
I think that one way to
manage that is to really try

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.545
to have kids lead the
conversation by asking questions.

00:10:29.545 --> 00:10:31.168
That way, you can answer their questions,

00:10:31.168 --> 00:10:34.560
hopefully satisfactorily
without providing perhaps

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:35.860
more information than they're seeking

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.990
which could inadvertently
make them more overwhelmed

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:40.774
or anxious by the situation.

00:10:40.774 --> 00:10:43.170
But I think it's important to shed light

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.180
on a situation for kids.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:49.050
And also help them understand
that things that you're doing

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:52.453
are intended to make them feel safe.

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:55.570
This is a problem that
adults are working on

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:58.580
but there are concrete
things that kids can do

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:01.120
like staying out of school right now,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.159
washing their hands frequently
and following those rules

00:11:04.159 --> 00:11:06.540
as a way to really help
keep themselves safe

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.416
which makes them feel as
though they have some control

00:11:08.416 --> 00:11:09.967
over the situation.

00:11:09.967 --> 00:11:13.250
- And Dave, what's your
sense of these things?

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.604
I know we talked about
it a couple days ago,

00:11:15.604 --> 00:11:18.570
how do you think about coping
with some of these things?

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:21.190
And I have this other question
from someone who's talking

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:24.360
about having trouble
concentrating in studies

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.145
because of social anxiety.

00:11:26.145 --> 00:11:31.145
How does that play out in
this type of a situation?

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:34.330
Dave, what do you think about that?

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.400
- Right I mean, I think
that you know, if we talk

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.180
about anxiety for one sec.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.340
And that's just,
anxiety's often brought on

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.340
by new situations.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.626
New things that are unexpected,

00:11:42.626 --> 00:11:44.345
things that you haven't
encountered before.

00:11:44.345 --> 00:11:46.728
So kids who have been socially anxious

00:11:46.728 --> 00:11:48.647
and even have resolved it to some degree

00:11:48.647 --> 00:11:50.146
may find themselves newly anxious

00:11:50.146 --> 00:11:54.270
in a midst of a kind of new landscape

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:55.830
where they're wondering
when they should jump in

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.558
an online classes or
they're wondering how public

00:11:57.558 --> 00:11:59.887
their comment might be
or whether or not others

00:11:59.887 --> 00:12:02.310
are chatting about them in the background.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:04.760
There could be a host of new
fears associated with that.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.209
And in reality, the way
we help kids with that

00:12:07.209 --> 00:12:10.864
is we help them to think
about the fact that whatever

00:12:10.864 --> 00:12:13.690
they might be afraid of you
know, we try to restructure

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.370
the moment that even what
they're afraid of the most

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.480
might not be that terrible.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.589
And in reality, the things they care about

00:12:20.589 --> 00:12:21.690
may still be happening.

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.241
And then we also try to
engage in exposure with them

00:12:24.241 --> 00:12:26.810
where we say, "Look, it may
mean that you're worried

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:28.269
about how a comment might be perceived

00:12:28.269 --> 00:12:30.294
but I need you to make two per class.

00:12:30.294 --> 00:12:32.112
And then let's go back
to the evidence and see

00:12:32.112 --> 00:12:35.660
how tough that was or how
anxious that made you."

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.190
And over time, we can get somebody

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:39.329
from perhaps being anxious
to even participate

00:12:39.329 --> 00:12:42.177
up to what their level of
participation might have been

00:12:42.177 --> 00:12:44.783
before online education or COVID.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:48.954
- Yeah, and more questions here.

00:12:48.954 --> 00:12:52.740
There's, one student is
talking about how do they deal,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.487
they're having depression at the same time

00:12:54.487 --> 00:12:57.378
they have a SAT and a
lot of studies to do.

00:12:57.378 --> 00:13:02.060
What advice would you
give for them, either?

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.790
- I think Laura and I can
both hit on different parts

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:05.623
of this question.

00:13:05.623 --> 00:13:08.900
But I think it's something
where when we see kids

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.660
who are preparing for the
SATs, what we're thinking about

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:13.780
and I'll go back to a point
that Laura made earlier

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:16.157
which is just about chunking.

00:13:16.157 --> 00:13:19.105
We know that somebody
whose mood might be low

00:13:19.105 --> 00:13:22.300
need to not think about
the entirety of preparation

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:25.090
for the SAT but rather you
know, think about it just

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:26.790
what they're gonna get done today.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.303
And then how they're
gonna reward themselves

00:13:28.303 --> 00:13:30.606
with something that's what we
call behaviorally activating

00:13:30.606 --> 00:13:33.120
which means that we know that
it's gonna improve their mood

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:34.210
to some degree.

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:36.572
So we try to plan out with
teenagers, do the work first,

00:13:36.572 --> 00:13:38.876
put in manageable chunk
and likely a smaller chunk

00:13:38.876 --> 00:13:42.288
than pre COVID then figure out
how you're gonna do something

00:13:42.288 --> 00:13:45.330
for your mood and something
to fell connected you know,

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:48.563
afterwards just to keep everything
kind of in that variety.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:53.650
- Yeah, I would just
add I mean, I don't know

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.690
if this is preexisting
depression but certainly

00:13:55.690 --> 00:14:00.690
social isolation and the lack
of accessibility of things

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:04.700
that make us feel good, lowers our mood.

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:07.300
So another thing that I try
to encourage parents to do

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:10.010
is to the extent that they
can, really try to mimic

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.720
their normal life but
with sticking to somewhat

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.142
of a typical routine.

00:14:16.142 --> 00:14:19.050
And finding ways, even
if you can't go outside

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.970
and have play dates with
friends face to face,

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:26.448
trying to schedule virtual
play dates, making sure

00:14:26.448 --> 00:14:29.333
that you're scheduling
time for outdoor activity

00:14:29.333 --> 00:14:32.060
if you're in a location where
you're allowed to go outside.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:33.850
But certainly physical activity we know,

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:38.550
has mood boosting effects and
learning boosting effects.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.751
So I think certainly scheduling in time

00:14:40.751 --> 00:14:45.326
for physical exercise and
outdoor time is really critical

00:14:45.326 --> 00:14:50.326
both to support learning
and to support mood.

00:14:50.716 --> 00:14:54.610
And again, really trying
to keep the days as close

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.070
to a typical day as you can.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:15:01.070
I heard recently about a
father who has a student

00:15:02.765 --> 00:15:07.270
who's on the autism
spectrum and takes his son

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:09.280
for a drive every morning as though

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:10.570
he were driving him to school.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:15.270
It's just a way to maintain
some semblance of normalcy

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.169
for him and it really helps
him get into the mindset

00:15:17.169 --> 00:15:18.060
of being in school.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:20.740
So that may not apply to
the case of this student

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.210
with depression but really
trying to mimic things

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:28.210
that a typical day, is
helpful for students.

00:15:29.091 --> 00:15:30.819
- I will say one other thing--

00:15:30.819 --> 00:15:32.297
- And what advice would
you say, oh go head.

00:15:32.297 --> 00:15:34.240
- Oh, I was just gonna say,
I will say one other point.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.760
For kids who are struggling
with social anxiety

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:37.580
or depression, some of the
things that Dr. Phillips

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:40.655
and I are talking about are
not necessarily intuitive.

00:15:40.655 --> 00:15:43.110
And the great thing about
at least how organizations

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:45.690
are adapting to the
COVID crisis now is that

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.030
organizations like ours,
the Child Mind Institute

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:51.664
and others have really moved
to a telehealth kind of forum

00:15:51.664 --> 00:15:54.206
where not only are we
working with active patients

00:15:54.206 --> 00:15:58.410
but we'll also do brief
consultations on issues like this

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.612
just to ensure that families
have support during this time.

00:16:00.612 --> 00:16:03.830
Because you know, it's
not intuitive how to deal

00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:06.150
with this crisis in the context of kids

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.812
who are battling mental health disorders.

00:16:08.812 --> 00:16:10.840
- And we have a question
here from YouTube,

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:13.204
Priscilla Cruze is asking,
"My child does have ADHD.

00:16:13.204 --> 00:16:16.153
How much time is enough to
homeschool for a second grader

00:16:16.153 --> 00:16:18.460
without pushing them too far?

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:20.110
They're normally in school for six hours."

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:21.400
And I think this is a
question that all parents

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:24.193
are struggling with, is normally
school is six, seven hours

00:16:24.193 --> 00:16:26.929
but what is the minimum and
what's maybe the maximum

00:16:26.929 --> 00:16:29.263
for this type of a scenario?

00:16:30.776 --> 00:16:31.990
- It's a great question.

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:35.100
And again, I would encourage,
I think that the teachers

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.800
can be helpful in at least advising what--

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:41.423
I mean, if the teachers
have concrete tasks

00:16:41.423 --> 00:16:46.130
for students to complete,
it would be the goal

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:47.570
for students to get through that work.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:49.450
And you might wanna consult
with teachers about how long

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:52.235
they would expect students
to either be engaged

00:16:52.235 --> 00:16:54.317
in that work on a daily basis
or how long they would expect

00:16:54.317 --> 00:16:59.310
that packet to take for
the student to complete.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:01.801
And beyond that the thing is,

00:17:01.801 --> 00:17:04.996
especially with very young
students and I'm considering

00:17:04.996 --> 00:17:07.960
even a second grade student
a very young student,

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:12.580
so many things are educational
even if they're not

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:14.420
traditionally found in a classroom.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:18.360
So the silver lining, I mean
I think they're are a lot

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:21.298
of silver linings of this
crisis but there are other

00:17:21.298 --> 00:17:25.920
learning opportunities that
students might not otherwise

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.770
have exposure to.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:31.640
So we have students who
are now doing more chores

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:32.730
around the house and they're learning

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:36.574
how to help their parents
cook, and that's a lesson.

00:17:36.574 --> 00:17:39.130
There's math involved in that,
there's chemistry involved

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:41.706
in that, there's a lot of
executive functioning developing

00:17:41.706 --> 00:17:44.463
with organization and sequencing of steps

00:17:44.463 --> 00:17:48.275
and patience and perseverance
and frustration tolerance.

00:17:48.275 --> 00:17:50.638
So there's learning that's
taking place even if it's not

00:17:50.638 --> 00:17:54.034
traditional classroom type learning.

00:17:54.034 --> 00:17:59.034
Boredom is actually I think,
a very critical experience

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:02.239
for students to have that in our culture

00:18:02.239 --> 00:18:05.290
we don't have many opportunities to feel.

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:07.749
And we know that boredom
really is how kids develop

00:18:07.749 --> 00:18:09.410
and nurture their creativity.

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:11.982
So I think you're gonna see
a lot of skills coming out

00:18:11.982 --> 00:18:16.150
of this crisis that might
not have otherwise had

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:17.380
the opportunity to emerge.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:19.770
And I think that that's something
that I really want parents

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.198
to try to focus on to
the extent that they can.

00:18:22.198 --> 00:18:25.020
So even if they're not
engaged in traditionally

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:26.948
academic tasks, there are other ways

00:18:26.948 --> 00:18:29.686
for them to be learning right now.

00:18:29.686 --> 00:18:32.056
- All right, and that's useful.

00:18:32.056 --> 00:18:34.784
The last question for
today on this topic is

00:18:34.784 --> 00:18:39.711
from YouTube Ketrin Setumeong
is asking, "How can I manage

00:18:39.711 --> 00:18:42.119
being a perfectionist when I'm studying?

00:18:42.119 --> 00:18:44.540
It makes me stressed because
I always say to myself,

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:47.150
'I must get the best and do the best?'"

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:48.649
How do you cope with that?

00:18:48.649 --> 00:18:51.580
And this is something
adults and children I think,

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:52.830
struggle with or many do.

00:18:55.210 --> 00:18:57.043
- I'll start it this way,
I think one of the things

00:18:57.043 --> 00:19:00.320
that we talk about
perfectionism and young people

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.890
or students of any age
right now is that you know,

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:05.520
the landscape has truly changed.

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:08.083
Many colleges and high
schools are trying to adapt

00:19:08.083 --> 00:19:11.946
their grading systems right
now to what's going on

00:19:11.946 --> 00:19:13.869
in online education and
may not be able to grade

00:19:13.869 --> 00:19:15.510
in exactly the same way.

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:18.410
And of course, for people
who already were concerned

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:21.008
about grades or wanted
their work to be perfect,

00:19:21.008 --> 00:19:23.040
that also means that the landscape

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:24.270
for their assessment's changing.

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:26.234
That can lead to a lot of anxiety.

00:19:26.234 --> 00:19:27.890
You know, one of the things that I think

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:30.670
we're speaking about a lot
with families and educators

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:32.824
right now is that as we
all adapt to this crisis

00:19:32.824 --> 00:19:35.879
it's good to kind of
capitalize on what we think

00:19:35.879 --> 00:19:39.224
is going well or what
quality work we think others

00:19:39.224 --> 00:19:43.253
are producing and not to expect
perfect or the same level

00:19:43.253 --> 00:19:45.266
of work that we would've
seen prior to this

00:19:45.266 --> 00:19:46.400
because people are just dealing

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:48.030
with so many more challenges.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:50.610
So again, you know, I think we'll go back

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:52.820
to an earlier point which is communicating

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:54.770
with your teachers about
what's expected on assignments

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:57.150
is a good thing.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:58.791
But many folks who are
proud of perfectionism,

00:19:58.791 --> 00:20:00.873
we're also trying to
include the coping thought

00:20:00.873 --> 00:20:03.210
that people are more empathic right now,

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:04.770
they're more compassionate,
they're more willing

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.751
to possibly grade you
positively because they know

00:20:07.751 --> 00:20:10.890
that maybe you're dealing with
more than you would usually.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:13.050
So it's not something
where every assignment

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:14.310
has to be perfect, you may actually have

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.078
a little bit more space, going
back to Dr. Phillips's point

00:20:16.078 --> 00:20:18.733
about silver linings for creativity.

00:20:19.794 --> 00:20:20.948
- That's super useful.

00:20:20.948 --> 00:20:23.403
You know, we've gotten questions
even before this crisis

00:20:23.403 --> 00:20:25.687
about things like
procrastination and you know,

00:20:25.687 --> 00:20:28.040
and one of the causes of
procrastination is the fear

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:30.377
that maybe you aren't
able to do it as much

00:20:30.377 --> 00:20:31.899
as you thought or it might be harder.

00:20:31.899 --> 00:20:34.302
But with kind of, whether
you call it perfectionism

00:20:34.302 --> 00:20:37.252
or procrastination, it's really
you know, just get started.

00:20:38.344 --> 00:20:42.380
And then that motion might help
unblock some of the things.

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:44.493
Well Laura, Dave, thanks
so much for joining today.

00:20:44.493 --> 00:20:46.733
This was super useful.

00:20:47.911 --> 00:20:51.550
I think a lot of people got
some really interesting insights

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:53.343
into this, thank you.

00:20:53.343 --> 00:20:55.456
- Thanks for having us.

00:20:55.456 --> 00:20:56.884
- Great, stay well.

00:20:56.884 --> 00:21:00.009
- So thanks everyone for joining today.

00:21:00.009 --> 00:21:04.901
You know, I'll just give a
reminder that this is a place

00:21:04.901 --> 00:21:08.180
for y'all to stay connected in this time

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:09.510
of social distancing.

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:12.120
You know, send us requests,
send us questions,

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:15.537
send us even folks that you
might wanna see on this forum

00:21:15.537 --> 00:21:16.927
and we'll try to reach out to them.

00:21:16.927 --> 00:21:20.270
I think a lot of people are
out there in the world today

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:21.870
trying to understand how they can help,

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:24.780
how they can help you as
parents, students and teachers.

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.430
I will remind everyone
again, we are not for profit.

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:29.390
If you're in a position,
please think about donating

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:30.470
to Khan Academy.

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:32.190
It's support like that that allows us

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:33.800
to support many other folks.

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:35.930
One of the things that
you could expect to see

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:38.520
over the next couple of days,
and actually we've already

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.243
released it a little bit, you're
gonna see a little bit more

00:21:40.243 --> 00:21:43.186
of the next day or two is
we released those schedules

00:21:43.186 --> 00:21:47.574
for students of different
age groups three weeks ago.

00:21:47.574 --> 00:21:50.070
And we got a lot of
positive feedback from y'all

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:52.133
that it was useful to structure
the day along the lines

00:21:52.133 --> 00:21:55.687
that we were just hearing
from, from our guest experts.

00:21:55.687 --> 00:21:59.188
What we've launching
right now, today, tomorrow

00:21:59.188 --> 00:22:00.900
are actually learning plans.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:05.740
Now that it's becoming clear
that school is going to be

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:08.434
out of session probably through
the end of the school year,

00:22:08.434 --> 00:22:11.605
students are trying to figure
out and parents and teachers,

00:22:11.605 --> 00:22:13.900
what could be done not
just over those two months

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.880
but potentially through the
summer so you don't have

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:19.860
five months of not just only
not learning but learning loss.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.792
And so expect to see some
links for learning plans

00:22:21.792 --> 00:22:24.414
on how you can space yourself
out, especially in math.

00:22:24.414 --> 00:22:26.380
If you're a sixth
grader, a seventh grader,

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:29.050
an algebra student, a
third grader, to make sure

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:30.708
that you are operating
at grade level and beyond

00:22:30.708 --> 00:22:32.730
as we go into the new school year.

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:34.570
And tomorrow and the
next few lives streams

00:22:34.570 --> 00:22:35.882
we'll go into a lot more depth on that.

00:22:35.882 --> 00:22:38.663
We just published a YouTube
video for those of y'all

00:22:38.663 --> 00:22:39.778
that are on YouTube.

00:22:39.778 --> 00:22:41.927
It's actually the latest
video that we just made public

00:22:41.927 --> 00:22:44.848
on our YouTube channel
where if you look at,

00:22:44.848 --> 00:22:47.500
it explains what these
learning plans look like

00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:48.991
and it has links in the description

00:22:48.991 --> 00:22:50.840
to the various learning plans.

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:51.960
And we're going to be posting it

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:53.784
on other social media shortly.

00:22:53.784 --> 00:22:56.414
So with that, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:22:56.414 --> 00:22:59.020
This is just a way for all
of us to stay connected

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:01.213
and stay healthy and stay safe.

